low us to deal with the  lifetime  of the fluorescence from the dyes as  well as the color of the fluorescence  what that's allowed us to do then is to  go after  the fret in a way that's much more  high fidelity i guess i should say by  being able to remove the background we  get a much greater dynamic  range by using the lifetime we're able  to use  tools that friend that that the  laboratory for fluorescence dynamics at  uc irvine enrico graton's unit  has been able to define that lets us get  a much better handle on what fraction of  the molecules are doing for that  as well as the strength of the fret the  potential distance between the two  fluorochromes  so it the the multi-spectral and  enabled by lifetime imaging or  spectrophleum as we would like to call  it  really gives you some tremendous ability  to go  after fret indicators and lifetime  indicators  in ways that we couldn't before anybody  that's interested in that  should really look at the website that  the laboratory of fluorescence dynamics  has at uc irvine because it's just  tremendous resource  to be able to use their analytical tools  and that sort of analysis  well scott i think we're going to have  to wrap it up there  i thank you for an excellent  presentation very exciting  technologies and um  if you have any other questions please  reach out to scott  and um send him an email or  reach out to him inside through the chat  so thanks  could i just say one thing in response  to a couple of the questions that have  come in  that we're having to cut off and one of  them is how long can we image and we've  imaged for days long  periods so these techniques are not  highly photo bleaching  and so this is something that be useful  in both  organoid settings and in high content  settings  where you don't want to perturb the  the tissues at all so  please reach out to me if you have  questions and i'd be happy to  um to answer you one on one  thanks again scott really appreciate it  thank you
 

